! Four citation tones + neutral tone

Mandarin's tone is lexically contrastive. Hanyu Pinyin marks four citation tones: mā (high-level), má (rising), mǎ (low/dipping), mà (falling), and an unstressed neutral tone (ma).

''Tone marks belong on every newly taught form in this wiki.'' Neutral-tone syllables normally appear without a tone mark.

!! Tone sandhi
Two adjacent third-tone syllables are commonly realized as a second tone followed by a third tone: 你好 (nǐ hǎo) is usually pronounced [ní hǎo] in connected speech. The spelling remains ''nǐ hǎo''.

!! Distinction to hear
* 妈 — mā — mother
* 马 — mǎ — horse

''Audio pilot:'' [[Audio Lab]] includes a recorded greeting illustrating third-tone sandhi.

''Note:'' voice synthesis is useful for exposure, but not proof that the learner produced the tones accurately.
